\begin{abstract}
  Memory mechanisms for embodied LLM agents have evolved from passive repositories into active, action-conditioned systems for long-horizon reasoning. However, extending these capabilities to decentralized multi-agent settings remains challenging. Partial observability and distributed control demand that agents minimize redundancy and mitigate conflicts without centralized supervision. We introduce Memory-as-Skill, a decentralized hierarchical framework that models individual and cooperative skills as persistent, retrievable abstractions grounded in memory primitives. Our approach distills reusable skills from inter-agent interactions and organizes them hierarchically. At execution time, a context-aware planner dynamically retrieves and composes these skills based on semantic context and subtask requirements, enabling coordination through shared behavioral abstractions rather than explicit messaging. Empirical evaluations demonstrate significant improvements over baselines in task success rates, completion ratios, and coordination efficiency across diverse task compositions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Large language model agents are demonstrating remarkable capabilities in increasingly sophisticated missions in various domains~\citep{aghzal2025survey, cao2025large}. Instead of serving as mere command-responders, these agents now act as persistent planners, capable of conducting research projects~\citep{zheng2025deepresearcher} and navigating complex real-world environments~\citep{ahn2022can} with long horizons~\citep{erdogan2025plan}.%
