\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

Memory-as-Skill operates in two stages: memory building and memory utilization. We first describe how the hierarchical skill library is constructed, then detail the retrieval mechanism.



\subsection{Memory Building Stage}
\label{subsec:memory_building}

Our framework organizes learned knowledge into a hierarchical skill library $\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{ind}} \cup \mathcal{L}_{\text{coop}}$, partitioned into individual skills (single-agent competencies) and cooperation skills (multi-agent coordination patterns). The term ``skill'' here refers to a reusable behavioral pattern in natural language, not executable code or API calls.

A \emph{skill} is a tuple $s = (\texttt{name}, \texttt{type}, \mathcal{I}_s)$, where \texttt{name} is a semantic identifier (e.g., \texttt{Pickup}, \texttt{Handover}) serving as the retrieval key, \texttt{type} $\in \{\texttt{individual}, \texttt{cooperation}\}$, and $\mathcal{I}_s$ is a set of grounded instances. An \emph{individual} instance is $(\texttt{context}, \texttt{demo}, \texttt{self\_cond})$, where \texttt{context} parameterizes the environment (e.g., \texttt{(Kitchen, Plate)}), \texttt{demo} is a natural-language demonstration, and \texttt{self\_cond} is a requirement on the agent's own state (e.g., ``my hand is free''). A \emph{cooperation} instance adds \texttt{partner\_cond}, an expected partner behavioral pattern inferred from environmental changes (e.g., ``partner picked up an object and approaching''). This separation between abstract skill (\emph{what to do}) and contextualized instance (\emph{where and on what to do it}) lets agents transfer learned skills to novel objects, locations, and partner behaviors not seen during memory building.

\paragraph{Effect-based Partner Inference.}\label{subsec:effect_inference}
\texttt{partner\_cond} indexes cooperation skills by the environmental effect of a partner's action rather than by the action token or partner identity. When agent $j$ observes $\Delta o_t^j$ such as ``object no longer on table'', it retrieves skills whose effect pattern matches---a behavioral abstraction that aligns with the functional view of Theory of Mind in~\citet{kosinski2023theory}. Because the retrieval key is task-agnostic, the same cooperation skill activates across unseen object--location combinations.

\paragraph{Skill Generation.}
We extract skills from successful trajectories using an LLM-based pipeline (Figure~\ref{fig:workflow}, Stage~1). The LLM segments trajectories at subgoal-completion boundaries and generates each skill's \texttt{name}, \texttt{context}, \texttt{demo}, and conditions. Cooperation skills are additionally identified at segments where an agent's action directly follows partner-induced environmental changes, with \texttt{partner\_cond} summarizing the triggering environmental delta.

\subsection{Memory Utilization Stage}
\label{subsec:utilization}
\label{subsec:memory_utilization}

At execution time, we leverage the alternating-round execution for implicit coordination: although partner actions are not directly observable, their environmental consequences provide sufficient signal for complementary response selection.

\paragraph{Hierarchical Skill Retrieval.}
Under partial observability, at time step $t$, agent $j$ cannot directly observe agent $i$'s action $a^i_t$ but infers partner behavior from the environmental change $\Delta o_{t}^j = f_{\mathsf{diff}}(o_{t-1}^j, o_t^j)$, where $f_{\mathsf{diff}}$ prompts the LLM to summarize task-relevant differences (e.g., ``the mug is no longer on the table''). Conditioning on $\Delta o_t^j$, the agent applies Theory of Mind reasoning to predict a skill name and retrieve relevant instances:
\begin{equation}
\hat{s}_t^j = f_{\mathsf{predict}}(\tau_t^j, \Delta o_{t}^j, I), \qquad
s^* = \arg\max_{s \in \mathcal{L}} \text{sim}(\hat{s}^j_t, \texttt{name}(s)), \qquad
\mathcal{I}_{\text{retrieved}} = \text{top-}k_{i \in \mathcal{I}_{s^*}}\{\text{sim}(\text{context}(i), o^j_t)\},
\end{equation}
where $\tau_t^j$ is agent $j$'s trajectory, $\text{sim}(\cdot,\cdot)$ is cosine similarity over sentence embeddings, and $\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{ind}} \cup \mathcal{L}_{\text{coop}}$. The two-stage design first selects the relevant skill category by name (\texttt{Pickup} vs.\ \texttt{Handover}, etc.) and then retrieves contextually appropriate demonstrations within it, balancing semantic fuzziness with grounded specificity.


\paragraph{Executability Filtering and Action Selection.}
Retrieved instances are filtered by executability via LLM-based verification: individual skills require only the agent's self-condition, $\mathcal{I}_{\text{exec}} = \{i \mid \texttt{self\_cond}_i(o_t^j)\}$, while cooperation skills additionally require that the observation difference matches the expected partner pattern, $\mathcal{I}_{\text{exec}} = \{i \mid \texttt{self\_cond}_i(o_t^j) \land \texttt{partner\_cond}_i(\Delta o_t^j)\}$. LLM-based verification (rather than rule-based) provides the semantic flexibility needed under distribution shift, since enumerating all objects, locations, and configurations in advance is infeasible. The top-ranked executable instances serve as in-context examples for action generation; because the agent observes concrete partner-induced outcomes rather than predicting partner behavior, action selection is grounded in actual task progress. When the predicted skill name fails to match any library entry above a calibrated embedding similarity threshold (Appendix~\ref{sec:threshold_calibration}), the agent falls back to zero-shot generation conditioned on $\tau_t^j$.

\paragraph{Implicit Coordination via Effect Inference.}
The combination of sequential execution and effect-conditioned cooperation skills yields coordination without explicit communication or partner-belief modeling. When agent $j$ observes $\Delta o_t^j$ at step $t$ (e.g., ``monitor stand no longer on the bedroom table''), the retrieved cooperation skill prescribes a complementary action (e.g., ``move the laptop stand'') rather than redundant re-execution; the environmental delta is itself a sufficient signal that the partner has claimed an overlapping subtask. This realizes Theory of Mind functionally in the sense of~\citet{kosinski2023theory}, but instantiates it as an effect-to-skill lookup rather than as an explicit partner-belief module---a representational choice with two practical consequences. First, the coordination signal is causal in direction: $\Delta o_t^j$ reflects what the partner has \emph{already done}, so action selection is grounded in observed task progress rather than in speculative inference over partner intent. Second, because cooperation skills are indexed by effect patterns rather than partner identity, the same skill activates across unseen partner pairings and constraint compositions---a property that anticipates the conflict reductions and asymmetric-deployment effects in Section~\ref{subsec:coordination}.


