We evaluate our Memory-as-Skill method on embodied multi-agent cooperation tasks, addressing three research questions:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em, itemsep=0.2em, topsep=0.2em, parsep=0pt]
    \item \textbf{RQ1:} Can memory-augmented LLM agents generalize cooperative behaviors from elementary tasks to novel composite task scenarios?
    \item \textbf{RQ2:} Which memory components are essential, and how should retrieval be routed across heterogeneous skill libraries?
    \item \textbf{RQ3:} Does skill-structured memory reduce coordination failures, and how should memory be deployed across agents to minimize them?
\end{itemize}




\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{subsec:setup}

\paragraph{Task Types.}
We evaluate on the PartNR benchmark~\citep{chang2024partnr}, featuring two-agent embodied cooperation in simulated household environments with sequential action execution. We classify tasks by four elementary types derived from evaluation propositions. \textbf{Rearrange (R)} requires object repositioning with location constraints (\texttt{is\_on\_top}, \texttt{is\_in\_room}). \textbf{Spatial (S)} requires explicit spatial relationships (\texttt{is\_next\_to}). \textbf{Heterogeneous (H)} involves state-changing actions (\texttt{is\_clean}, \texttt{is\_filled}, \texttt{is\_powered\_on}). \textbf{Temporal (T)} indicates sequential dependencies via DAG edges. Composite types (R\_S, H\_R, H\_R\_T, H\_R\_S\_T) combine multiple constraints. Validated against ground-truth labels on 2{,}000 PartNR episodes, our proposition-based classification achieves F1=0.97 on elementary types and F1=0.90 on composite types (Appendix~\ref{sec:task_classification_appendix}, Figure~\ref{fig:task_classification}).


\paragraph{Baselines.}
We compare against four baselines. (1) \textbf{Zero-Shot}: LLM agents without memory, relying solely on in-context reasoning. (2) \textbf{ToM}~\citep{li2023theory}: explicit partner belief modeling through a dedicated reasoning module. (3) \textbf{RAG}~\citep{lewis2020retrieval}: retrieval-augmented generation using semantic similarity to retrieve successful trajectories from a training database. (4) \textbf{MEMENTO}~\citep{kwon2025embodied}: structured trajectory-based memory with knowledge graph organization. For fair comparison, all memory-based methods use the same trajectory data collected from heuristic agent~\citep{chang2024partnr} rollouts, with our memories extracted using LLaMA-3.1-70B.

\paragraph{Models and Metrics.}
We evaluate with six LLM backbones: Llama 3.1-8B, Llama 3.3-70B, Qwen 2.5-7B, Qwen 2.5-72B, Claude 3.5 Sonnet, and GPT-4o. Metrics include Success Rate (fraction of fully completed episodes), Completion Percentage (average subtask completion), and coordination failures (object contention, spatial conflicts). We use notation Task(Memory) for configurations, e.g., H\_R\_S\_T(R+S) denotes evaluation on a 4-constraint compositional task using memory from R-only and S-only elementary tasks.

\paragraph{Evaluation Protocol.}
Memory is built using LLaMA-3.1-70B from heuristic-agent rollouts and remains frozen during all evaluation, yielding 7--280 individual and 47--269 cooperation skills per memory subset (Appendix~\ref{sec:skill_memory_analysis}). Per-task evaluation episodes are 76 for R\_S, up to 197 for H\_R, 11--42 for H\_R\_T, and 5--15 for H\_R\_S\_T, reflecting PartNR's compositional design where high-constraint tasks are inherently rarer. Across all backbone-method-memory configurations, our evaluation totals over 14{,}000 episode runs across 205 configurations, the largest evaluation in the embodied multi-agent memory literature to our knowledge.

\subsection{Main Results}
\label{subsec:main_results}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results_task} presents task performance across all methods and LLM backbones on Rearrange+Spatial tasks, where memory-based methods use Rearrange-only memory to evaluate cross-type transfer capability.



Our method consistently outperforms baselines across most model configurations. On Llama-8B, we achieve 72.2\% success compared to 71.7\% for MEMENTO and 66.7\% for ToM. The improvement margin increases with model scale: on Llama-70B, our method achieves 88.2\% versus 85.4\% for ToM, representing a 3.3\% relative improvement. On Qwen-7B, which struggles most with these tasks, our method achieves 57.4\% success compared to 42.1\% for MEMENTO, a 36\% relative improvement, indicating that the hierarchical skill structure provides substantial benefits for weaker models. GPT-4o is the one exception (70.2\% success vs.\ 78.7\% zero-shot, but $86.7\%$ completion---negative transfer on success that we attribute to retrieved demonstrations over-constraining a strong reasoner that already excels with explicit ToM prompting at $81.6\%$; further analysis in Appendix~\ref{sec:per_backbone}).

The aggregate gain in Table~\ref{tab:main_results_task} prompts three diagnostic questions: (i) where do the gains come from across task complexity (RQ1, Section~\ref{subsec:generalization}); (ii) which memory components carry them (RQ2, Section~\ref{subsec:ablation}); and (iii) how do they manifest in inter-agent dynamics (RQ3, Section~\ref{subsec:coordination}). Quantitative breakdowns supporting each RQ are provided in Appendix~\ref{sec:failure_scenarios_appendix}--\ref{sec:skill_memory_analysis}.

\subsection{Compositional Generalization (RQ1)}
\label{subsec:generalization}

To answer \textbf{RQ1}, we evaluate whether agents can transfer skills learned from single-constraint elementary tasks to multi-constraint composite tasks not encountered during memory building.

Figure~\ref{fig:combined_results}(a) compares success rates across tasks with increasing constraint complexity. On 2-constraint tasks, zero-shot achieves 50\% on R\_S but only 19.3\% on the more challenging H\_R transfer, while our method reaches 85.9\% with combined R+S memory, representing a 35.9\% absolute improvement. The performance gap widens on 3-constraint tasks (H\_R\_T), where zero-shot completely fails and our method achieves 27.7\% with H\_R+H\_T memory, outperforming RAG (11.1\%) and MEMENTO (4.5\%). On the most challenging 4-constraint tasks (H\_R\_S\_T), our method attains 40\% with R-only memory, doubling MEMENTO's performance (n$=$5 evaluation episodes; absolute percentages reflect 1--2 episode differences). These results demonstrate that task complexity creates a critical threshold where zero-shot performance collapses, underscoring the necessity of memory-augmented approaches. Moreover, compositional memory retrieval consistently outperforms single-type retrieval, validating that structured skill decomposition enables more effective knowledge transfer in complex multi-agent coordination scenarios. The transfer-quality dependence on retrieval count $k$ is examined in Appendix~\ref{subsec:topk}, where harder transfers (R$\rightarrow$HRST) peak at $k{=}5$ and degrade sharply beyond it.




