\subsection{Coordination Failures and Memory Deployment (RQ3)}
\label{subsec:coordination}

To answer \textbf{RQ3}, we measure how skill-structured memory affects coordination, first by quantifying failures relative to a strong memory baseline, then by asking how memory should be \emph{deployed} across agents to minimize those failures.

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figs/coop_heatmap_2x2.pdf}
    \caption{Coordination failures ($\downarrow$): ours vs.\ MEMENTO on Llama-3.1-8B. Cells show our failure count and improvement \% (green: fewer, red: more). Conflict, FailPick, SelfConf, NotClose; definitions in Appendix~\ref{appendix:failure_metrics}.}
    \label{fig:coop_heatmap}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:coop_heatmap} compares our method against MEMENTO on conflicts per episode (Conf/Ep), failed pick attempts (FailPick/Ep), self-confusion errors (SelfConf/Ep), and navigation failures (NotClose/Ep). Our method reduces inter-agent conflicts (Conflict/Ep) by up to 91\% on H\_R(R) and by 37\% to 71\% on 3-constraint H\_R\_T tasks. Self-conflict errors (SelfConf/Ep) decrease by 75\% to 97\% on simpler transfers, showing that our method provides more coherent action guidance. Navigation failures (NotClose/Ep) also improve by 33\% to 87\% across most configurations. For 4-constraint H\_R\_S\_T tasks, our method maintains competitive performance on Conflict/Ep and NotClose/Ep, while FailPick/Ep and SelfConf/Ep show room for improvement when handling multiple heterogeneous constraints simultaneously.

\paragraph{Specific Failure Scenarios on 4-Constraint Tasks.}
On H\_R\_S\_T, three failure modes emerge from Figure~\ref{fig:coop_heatmap}: missing manipulation skills under H\_T memory drive a $+344\%$ increase in FailPick/Ep; spatial-reasoning gaps under H\_R memory drive a $+302\%$ increase in SelfConf/Ep; and identical temporal cues under H\_T memory drive a $+19\%$ increase in Conflict/Ep, as both agents infer overlapping subtasks from the same environmental delta (full breakdown in Appendix~\ref{sec:failure_scenarios_appendix}). The third mode is structurally distinct: failure originates not from missing memory but from \emph{symmetric} memory producing conflicting partner inferences. We test this hypothesis next.

\paragraph{Memory Deployment Across Agents.}
A one-agent ablation, in which only one agent uses Memory-as-Skill while the other operates zero-shot, reveals three findings on Llama 3.1-8B (Figure~\ref{fig:one_agent}). First, the leader (Agent~0) benefits more from memory than the follower across all task groups; on H\_R, Agent~0 only (0.496 success) exceeds even the both-memory configuration (0.223). Second, follower-only memory is consistently counterproductive: Agent~1 only yields the weakest performance everywhere, indicating that \texttt{partner\_cond} expectations become misaligned when the leader lacks corresponding memory. Third, on R\_S, adding Agent~0 memory improves success by 0.151 versus 0.101 for Agent~1, consistent with the leader's role in the skill-prediction step.

