\end{figure}

The H\_R inversion is the sharpest case. When both agents retrieve cooperation skills, Conflict/Ep reaches 1.63, nearly five times that of Agent~0 only (0.33; mechanistic explanation and full failure-metric breakdown in Appendix~\ref{sec:hr_failure_appendix}). Both agents' \texttt{partner\_cond} expectations assume consistent partner behavior, but neither matches when both simultaneously deviate from zero-shot. Removing Agent~1's memory eliminates this mutual mismatch, halving conflicts and doubling success from 22.3\% to 49.6\%. NotClose/Ep also drops from 16.0 to 9.8. SelfConf/Ep rises slightly under one-agent conditions (1.36 vs.\ 0.27), but this self-confusion has far less impact on joint coordination than inter-agent conflict. Cooperation skills are thus most effective when concentrated in the coordinating agent, with the follower benefiting from adaptive flexibility---a role-aware deployment principle we revisit in Section~\ref{sec:conclusion}.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented Memory-as-Skill, a hierarchical memory framework for decentralized embodied multi-agent cooperation that separates abstract skills from contextualized instances and partitions knowledge into individual and cooperation-specific libraries. Across six LLM backbones and 14{,}000$+$ PartNR episodes, cooperation skills drive the largest share of the gains, and effect-based partner inference substantially reduces inter-agent conflicts relative to strong memory baselines. Indexing cooperation skills by environmental effects rather than partner identity activates a single skill across novel objects, locations, and unfamiliar partners, removing the need for explicit communication or partner-belief modeling. The same representational choice explains why cooperation memory should scale with agent role rather than count: matched partner-condition expectations become mutually inconsistent when both partners simultaneously deviate from a common reference.

\paragraph{Limitations and Future Work.}
Three limitations remain: domain transfer beyond PartNR's household environments is unverified, memory building requires successful trajectories (relaxable via learned policies or demonstrations), and effect-based inference exploits sequential execution, leaving concurrent execution as an open direction. Initial $N{=}3$ validation in Section~\ref{subsec:coordination} indicates that the role-aware deployment principle extends beyond the dyadic setting; broader $N$ and concurrent settings remain to be characterized.
