\paragraph{Why H\_R is the sharpest inversion case.} Among the task groups we evaluate, H\_R combines manipulation actions (Pickup, Place) with state-changing actions on the same set of objects (e.g., \texttt{is\_clean}, \texttt{is\_filled}). This co-occurrence produces the densest \texttt{partner\_cond} space: a single environmental delta $\Delta o_t^j$ can plausibly index multiple stored cooperation skills (the partner could be initiating a handover, completing a state change, or starting an adjacent subtask). When both agents condition on cooperation memory simultaneously, each independently selects from this dense candidate set, and the probability that their selections collide on the same object grows accordingly. Tasks with sparser cross-product structure (R\_S, where rearrangement and spatial constraints decouple cleanly) or asymmetric structure (H\_R\_T, where temporal ordering naturally serializes the agents) do not exhibit the same density, which is why the inversion is sharpest precisely at H\_R. This interpretation aligns with the SelfConf/Ep pattern in Table~\ref{tab:hr_failure}: under both-memory, self-confusion is rare (0.27)---each agent is internally consistent---but inter-agent Conflict/Ep dominates (1.63), exactly the signature of independent agents drawing from the same dense distribution.

\subsection{Extension to N>2 Agents}
\label{sec:n_agent_extension}

Section~\ref{subsec:coordination} establishes role-aware deployment in the 2-agent setting. We test whether this principle reflects a structural property of effect-based partner inference or an artifact of the dyadic setting by extending the framework to $N{=}3$.

\paragraph{Multi-effect attribution.} The 2-agent $f_{\mathsf{diff}}$ implicitly assumes each $\Delta o_t^j$ originates from the single other agent, an assumption that breaks when multiple partners may have caused observed environmental changes. We replace $f_{\mathsf{diff}}$ with a multi-effect attribution step. Given the raw observation diff and the recent positions and inventory states of all $N{-}1$ other agents, the planner LLM attributes each effect to the most likely source agent, and the retrieval pipeline conditions \texttt{partner\_cond} matching on the attributed agent rather than treating $\Delta o_t^j$ as a single signal. When attribution is ambiguous (e.g., two agents traversed the same room), the mechanism falls back to identity-agnostic retrieval, preserving the robustness of the original framework.

\paragraph{Evaluation setup.} We instantiate three agents on PartNR H\_R tasks with Llama-3.1-8B as the planner backbone, drawing from the same H\_R subset as the 2-agent ablation in Table~\ref{tab:hr_failure} with subgoal allocation distributed across three agents. Three deployment conditions are compared: (i) \textbf{All-mem}, all three agents use Memory-as-Skill; (ii) \textbf{Leader-only}, Agent~0 uses memory while Agents~1 and~2 operate zero-shot; (iii) \textbf{Followers-only}, Agents~1 and~2 use memory while Agent~0 operates zero-shot. Following the statistical-caveat framing of Appendix~\ref{subsec:threshold_setup}, absolute percentages serve as directional indicators while relative orderings between conditions are the more reliable claim.

\paragraph{Results.} Table~\ref{tab:n3_failure} reports per-condition success, completion, and the four coordination-failure metrics. Three patterns emerge.

\textit{The H\_R inversion sharpens at $N{=}3$.} Leader-only achieves $41.8\%$ success and $0.61$ Conflict/Ep, more than triple the All-mem success rate ($11.5\%$). All-mem Conflict/Ep rises from $1.63$ at $N{=}2$ (Table~\ref{tab:hr_failure}) to $2.58$ at $N{=}3$, consistent with the combinatorial growth in pairwise \texttt{partner\_cond} relationships from $\binom{2}{2}{=}1$ to $\binom{3}{2}{=}3$. The mechanism predicted in Appendix~\ref{sec:hr_failure_appendix}, where independent agents draw from the same dense \texttt{partner\_cond} distribution, operates at higher intensity when the symmetric-memory pool grows.

\textit{Followers-only outperforms All-mem but lags Leader-only.} The Followers-only success rate ($0.193$) sits between the two extremes. The two followers can leverage cooperation memory to coordinate with each other, but absent a memory-equipped leader the system loses the centralized skill-prediction step that drives effective task allocation in the 2-agent Leader-only condition. Step-count efficiency illustrates the cost: Followers-only requires roughly $3.7\times$ the simulator steps per episode compared with Leader-only, indicating that residual coordination among followers cannot compensate for the missing leader-level memory.

\textit{Symmetric memory induces planner instability.} A non-trivial fraction of All-mem episodes terminate with malformed planner output and are counted as failures in Table~\ref{tab:n3_failure}. The instability is consistent with the \texttt{partner\_cond} mismatch interpretation in Appendix~\ref{sec:hr_failure_appendix}: as $N$ increases, the conditional distributions over partner behavior that each agent must reconcile grow combinatorially, and the planner LLM's reasoning over conflicting partner expectations can degrade to invalid structured output, providing a behavioral signature of the structural failure mode.

