\subsection{LLM-based Threshold Calibration}
\label{sec:threshold_calibration}

We calibrate the embedding similarity threshold for skill memory retrieval using LLMs as judges. Rather than relying on heuristic thresholds, we employ four LLMs (Llama-3.1-8B, Llama-3.1-70B, Qwen-2.5-7B, Qwen-2.5-72B) to independently judge which candidate skills are relevant, then measure the embedding similarity scores of their selections to determine task-adaptive thresholds. \textbf{Note on calibration scope.} The threshold and retrieval-count analyses in this appendix and Appendix~\ref{subsec:topk} use the same evaluation episodes as the main experiments; we report them as sensitivity analyses to characterize the operating regime of the framework rather than as held-out hyperparameter tuning. The single global setting used throughout the main results (unified threshold $\theta=0.3$ and $k=5$) is fixed across all backbones and memory configurations, so the same hyperparameters apply uniformly to in-distribution and compositional out-of-distribution evaluation; future work should validate this regime on a separate held-out split.

\subsubsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{subsec:threshold_setup}

For each (evaluation task, memory bank) pair, we embed all task instructions and skill descriptions using \texttt{all-mpnet-base-v2}, retrieve the top-20 candidate skills by cosine similarity for each test episode, and present these candidates to each LLM via a structured prompt asking it to select the top-5 most relevant skills. We then sweep similarity thresholds (0.2--0.6) and compute Precision, Recall, and F1, treating LLM selections as ground truth. All four models achieve 100\% parse success rate across all configurations, ensuring reliable ground truth labels.

The evaluation spans four task types: Rearrange+Spatial (RS, 76 episodes), Heterogeneous+Rearrange (HR, 197 episodes), Heterogeneous+Rearrange+Temporal (HRT, 11 episodes), and the full combination (HRST, 5 episodes). Skills are retrieved from five memory banks: R (450 skills), S (20 skills), HR (455 skills), HT (12 skills), and HRST (32 skills).

\paragraph{Hardware and inference setup.} Open-weight LLM evaluation (Llama-3.1-8B/70B, Llama-3.3-70B, Qwen-2.5-7B/72B) is performed on compute nodes equipped with $4\times$ NVIDIA GH200 Grace Hopper Superchips, each providing an H100 Tensor Core GPU with 96~GB of HBM memory, a 72-core Grace CPU with 120~GB of LPDDR memory, and NVIDIA NVLink-C2C interconnect between CPU and GPU. The 70B-class models are loaded in BF16 and sharded across multiple GPUs within a node. GPT-4o is accessed through the OpenAI API. All wall-clock latency measurements reported in Appendix~\ref{subsec:empirical_latency} were collected on this hardware.

\paragraph{Computational Complexity.}
Per-agent per-step cost is $\mathcal{O}\big(C_{\text{LLM}} + (|\mathcal{L}| + K) \cdot d + k \cdot C_{\text{LLM}}\big)$, where $|\mathcal{L}|$ is the abstract-skill library size, $K$ is the number of grounded instances within the matched skill, $d$ is the embedding dimension, and $k$ is the retrieval count. The three terms correspond to skill prediction, embedding-based matching and retrieval, and executability filtering on $k$ candidates. No term depends on agent count $N$, and per-round wall-clock time scales as $\mathcal{O}(N \cdot C_{\text{LLM}})$ under sequential execution, in contrast to the $\mathcal{O}(N^2 \cdot C_{\text{LLM}})$ overhead of communication-based approaches.



\paragraph{Statistical caveat for high-constraint task groups.} The smaller episode counts on H\_R\_T (11) and H\_R\_S\_T (5) reflect PartNR's compositional design: the benchmark's task generator constructs tasks by combining elementary constraint types, and the joint distribution of $\geq$3 simultaneous constraints is inherently sparse in natural household instructions. Single-episode differences therefore translate to large percentage shifts on these subsets (e.g., on H\_R\_S\_T, $1/5$ episodes equals $20\%$ and $2/5$ equals $40\%$). Reported absolute percentages on 4-constraint tasks should be interpreted as directional indicators of the framework's compositional generalization capacity rather than as precise effect sizes; relative orderings between methods, which are robust under bootstrapping (the methods differ by $\geq 2$ episodes on H\_R\_S\_T), are the more reliable claim.

\subsubsection{Threshold Calibration Results}
\label{subsec:threshold_results}

We analyze F1 scores as a function of embedding similarity threshold to identify task-adaptive retrieval thresholds. Figure~\ref{fig:per_task_f1} presents the per-task F1 curves across all four LLMs, showing strong cross-model consensus on optimal thresholds.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{figs_append/fig1_per_task_threshold_f1.pdf}
    \caption{Per-task F1 curves across embedding similarity thresholds. Dashed red lines indicate the consensus threshold. Simple tasks (RS, HR) achieve optimal F1 at threshold 0.2, while complex tasks (HRT, HRST) peak at 0.4--0.45, demonstrating the need for task-adaptive thresholds.}
    \label{fig:per_task_f1}
\end{figure}

Three key observations emerge from this analysis. First, \textit{simple tasks benefit from lower thresholds}. RS and HR tasks achieve optimal F1 unanimously at threshold 0.2 across all four models. These tasks have rich matching candidates in the memory banks, allowing a lower threshold to capture relevant skills without excessive noise.

Second, \textit{complex tasks require higher thresholds}. HRT and HRST tasks achieve optimal F1 at thresholds 0.4--0.45, with 3--4 models in agreement. The additional temporal and spatial constraints necessitate more stringent matching criteria to filter out marginally related skills.

Third, \textit{cross-model consensus validates recommendations}. Despite differences in model family (Llama vs.\ Qwen) and scale (7B--72B), the threshold recommendations show strong agreement, particularly among the three larger models (8B, 70B, 72B).

\paragraph{Precision-Recall Trade-off.}
Figure~\ref{fig:prf1_detailed} provides a detailed breakdown of Precision, Recall, and F1 for each model-task combination. The curves reveal the underlying trade-off: lower thresholds maximize recall at the cost of precision, while higher thresholds improve precision but sacrifice recall. For simple tasks (RS, HR), the curves indicate that even at low thresholds, precision remains acceptable due to the abundance of relevant skills. For complex tasks (HRT, HRST), the steeper precision curves justify higher thresholds to filter noise effectively.

