\paragraph{Memory Bank Effects.}
Discrimination ability varies substantially across memory banks. Temporal skills (HT memory) exhibit the highest discrimination gaps (0.07--0.18), indicating that LLMs strongly distinguish temporal skill relevance. Combined skills (HRST memory) also show high gaps (0.06--0.22). In contrast, large rearrangement banks (R with 450 skills, HR with 455 skills) show lower gaps (0.01--0.04), reflecting densely packed embeddings where many skills have similar relevance scores.

\paragraph{Model Scale Effects.}
Larger models consistently demonstrate stronger discrimination ability. Llama-3.1-70B achieves the highest discrimination gap (up to 0.216 on HRST$\times$HRST), with Qwen-2.5-72B close behind (up to 0.207), while Qwen-7B shows the weakest discrimination with occasional negative gaps on cross-type pairs. This pattern suggests that model capacity directly influences the quality of skill relevance judgments.

