\subsubsection{Summary}
\label{subsec:threshold_summary}

Based on cross-model consensus, the calibration recommends task-adaptive thresholds: 0.2 for simple tasks (RS, HR) to maximize recall, and 0.4 for complex multi-constraint tasks (HRT, HRST) to improve precision. The strong agreement across four diverse LLMs validates that a principled threshold should adapt to task complexity. In our deployed system, however, we use a unified $\theta=0.3$ that falls within both calibrated regions for simplicity; because observed retrieval scores cluster well above $0.4$ across all task types (Section~\ref{subsec:fallback_rate}), this single-threshold simplification produces empirically equivalent fallback behavior to the task-adaptive setting (the per-task fallback rates would change by at most a few tenths of a percent under the recommended adaptive values).

\subsubsection{Fallback Activation Rate}
\label{subsec:fallback_rate}

To quantify how often the fallback path activates during evaluation, we instrument the retrieval pipeline and log every retrieval call in our main experiments. Across $8{,}855$ retrieval calls under the matched-memory configurations used for the main results, the fallback to retrieval-free generation triggered in $0$ cases ($0.00\%$ fallback rate; equivalently, $100\%$ memory utilization). Composite similarity scores remained well above the deployment threshold $\theta=0.3$ across all task types, with median post-boost scores of $0.836$ on R\_S, $0.813$ on H\_R, $1.162$ on H\_R\_T, and $1.226$ on H\_R\_S\_T (and minima of $0.488$, $0.343$, $0.845$, $0.580$ respectively---all above $\theta=0.3$). On a broader evaluation set spanning more diverse memory configurations ($13{,}795$ retrieval calls), the overall fallback rate remained at $0.55\%$, concentrating on the most challenging 4-constraint H\_R\_S\_T tasks ($2.65\%$, $27/1{,}020$) where library coverage is most stretched, while staying at $0\%$ on the H\_R and H\_R\_T groups.

To verify that the threshold mechanism activates correctly when the library genuinely lacks relevant skills, we ran a controlled stress test using S-only memory on H\_R\_S\_T tasks, deliberately misaligning memory content with task requirements. Fallback triggered in $90.0\%$ of retrieval calls ($27/30$), and similarly elevated on R\_S tasks evaluated against S-only memory ($3.8\%$, $49/1{,}283$), confirming that the safety mechanism functions as designed. The near-zero fallback rate observed in main experiments therefore reflects the strong alignment between the constructed skill library and PartNR's task distribution, not a degenerate threshold setting---memory is utilized in essentially every planning step under the configurations we report.

\subsubsection{Empirical Latency}
\label{subsec:empirical_latency}

To complement the asymptotic complexity analysis in Section~\ref{subsec:utilization}, we instrument the retrieval and generation pipeline on Llama-3.1-8B (H\_R tasks; $1{,}270$ steps for our method, $845$ for MEMENTO) and report wall-clock measurements collected via \texttt{time.perf\_counter()}.

\textbf{Retrieval is computationally negligible.} Per retrieval call, the pipeline averages approximately $54$~ms total: query-side embedding generation ($0.11$~ms), abstract skill matching over the library ($28.4$~ms), instance retrieval within the matched skill ($24.8$~ms), and executability filtering ($0.43$~ms). Amortized across all planning steps---retrieval is invoked only on skill transitions rather than every step---the per-step retrieval contribution is approximately $1.3$~ms ($\sim$$0.01\%$ of step latency).

\textbf{End-to-end step latency is longer than MEMENTO due to richer prompts.} Total per-step latency averages $9.4 \pm 9.0$~s for our method versus $3.7 \pm 1.8$~s for MEMENTO. Action generation accounts for over $99\%$ of step time in both methods. The roughly $2.6\times$ increase relative to MEMENTO is therefore not attributable to the retrieval mechanism but to longer in-context prompts: the $k=5$ retrieved skill demonstrations expand the LLM input, lengthening both prompt-encoding and (when chain-of-thought reasoning is triggered) completion time. The elevated variance for our method ($\sigma/\mu \approx 0.96$ vs.\ $0.50$ for MEMENTO) reflects occasional long reasoning traces on harder subtasks.

\textbf{Trade-off discussion.} Our framework targets compositional, long-horizon cooperation tasks where success rate, not latency, is the dominant deployment constraint. The per-step latency increase buys $1.5$--$2\times$ success-rate improvements on $3$- and $4$-constraint tasks (Section~\ref{subsec:generalization}), which we view as a favorable trade-off in this regime. For latency-sensitive deployments, the retrieval count $k$ can be reduced (Appendix~\ref{subsec:topk} shows $k{=}3$ retains most performance on simpler transfers at lower prompt cost), or executability filtering can be replaced with rule-based verification on closed object sets, both at the cost of some success rate.


