\caption{Effect of retrieval count $k$ on transfer performance, evaluated on Llama-3.1-8B. \textbf{(a)(b)} R-only memory transfer to four target task complexities: success and completion rates as functions of $k$. \textbf{(c)} Aligned memory: retrieved memory types match task requirements. \textbf{(d)} Misaligned memory: R-only memory used for all task types. Across panels, $k{=}5$ is the most consistent operating point; $k{=}20$ degrades complex transfers as excess retrieval introduces conflicting guidance.}
\label{fig:topk_ablation_append}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:topk_ablation_append} reports retrieval-count sensitivity across two views: line plots over $k \in \{3, 5, 10, 20\}$ for R-only transfer (panels a, b), and grouped bars comparing aligned and misaligned memory at the same $k$ values (panels c, d). For aligned R\_S tasks (panel c), success rates remain stable between 73.6\% and 85.9\% across all $k$, suggesting that when memory content matches task requirements the retrieval system consistently surfaces relevant skills regardless of the retrieved count.

Sensitivity to $k$ increases substantially under harder transfers and memory misalignment. On 3-constraint H\_R\_T (panel a), $k{=}5$ achieves optimal performance in most configurations, with H\_R\_T(H\_R+H\_T) reaching 27.7\% success at $k{=}5$ but dropping to 6.9\% at $k{=}20$. The 4-constraint H\_R\_S\_T transfer exhibits the sharpest sensitivity: H\_R\_S\_T(R) attains 40.0\% success at $k{=}5$ but only 15.4\% at $k{=}10$ (panel d). These results reveal a fundamental trade-off: insufficient $k$ misses relevant experiences, while excessive $k$ dilutes useful guidance with irrelevant or contradictory skills, particularly when retrieved instances span constraint types that suggest incompatible actions.

Completion rates (panel b) exhibit less sensitivity to $k$ than success rates, indicating that retrieval count primarily affects whether agents satisfy all task constraints simultaneously rather than their ability to make partial progress. Based on these findings, we set $k=5$ throughout our main experiments as the best balance between retrieval coverage and guidance coherence.


\subsection{Skill Memory Analysis}
\label{sec:skill_memory_analysis}

We analyze the hierarchical skill memory extracted by four LLM backbones (Llama-70B, Llama-8B, Qwen-7B, Qwen-72B) across five task categories: Rearrange-only (R), Spatial-only (S), Heterogeneous+Rearrange (HR), Heterogeneous+Temporal (HT), and the full combination (HRST). The skill memory comprises two levels: \textbf{individual skills} ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{ind}}$) representing single-agent primitives, and \textbf{cooperative skills} ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{coop}}$) capturing multi-agent coordination patterns.

\subsubsection{Skill Library Composition}
\label{subsec:skill_composition}

We first examine the overall scale and composition of extracted skill libraries across models. Figure~\ref{fig:skill_composition} presents the stacked distribution of individual and cooperative skills generated by each model across task types.

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figs_append/fig12_stacked_skills.pdf}
    \caption{Skill library composition across models and task types. Stacked bars show individual skills (solid) and cooperative skills (hatched). Larger models (Llama-70B, Qwen-72B) generate richer libraries, while smaller models produce more compact representations with varying individual-to-cooperative ratios.}
    \label{fig:skill_composition}
\end{figure}

Three key observations emerge from this analysis. First, \textit{larger models generate richer skill libraries}. Llama-70B produces the largest combined library, with 223 individual and 227 cooperative skills for the R task alone. In contrast, Qwen-7B generates the most compact library (72 individual, 48 cooperative for R), suggesting higher-level abstraction with fewer distinct skill types.

Second, \textit{the individual-to-cooperative skill ratio varies across model architectures}. Llama-70B maintains a balanced ratio ($\approx$1:1 for the R task), whereas Llama-8B exhibits a cooperative-heavy distribution (139 individual vs.\ 294 cooperative for R), indicating that the smaller model may group multi-step sequences into cooperative abstractions rather than decomposing them into primitives.

Third, \textit{task complexity inversely correlates with skill library size}. Simpler tasks (R, S) with more training episodes yield larger skill libraries, while complex heterogeneous tasks (HT, HRST) produce fewer but potentially more specialized skills. This pattern reflects the underlying data distribution rather than inherent task difficulty.

\subsubsection{Skill Taxonomy}
\label{subsec:skill_taxonomy}

To understand the semantic nature of extracted skills, we categorize the generated skills into functional groups. Figure~\ref{fig:skill_categories} presents the distribution of skill categories for both individual and cooperative skills across all four models.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{figs_append/fig5_skill_categories.pdf}
    \caption{Skill category distributions for individual skills (top row) and cooperative skills (bottom row) across models. Individual skills converge to common manipulation primitives (Navigation, Pick, Place), while cooperative skills exhibit greater divergence in abstraction patterns.}
    \label{fig:skill_categories}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Individual Skill Categories.}
All models converge on similar primitive action categories, suggesting a shared understanding of fundamental manipulation operations. Navigation skills dominate across all models (43--116 skills), followed by manipulation primitives including Pick/Retrieve and Place/Put. Llama-70B exhibits the most diverse distribution with substantial Move/Transport skills (98), while Qwen-7B produces a more focused library emphasizing Navigation (43) and State Change (25). This convergence indicates that individual skill extraction is relatively robust to model architecture and capacity.

\paragraph{Cooperative Skill Categories.}
In contrast, greater divergence emerges in how models abstract multi-agent coordination patterns. Joint Transport dominates for larger models (Llama-70B: 138, Qwen-72B: 145), reflecting collaborative object movement as the most frequently occurring coordination pattern in the dataset. Task-Specific Cooperation is prominent in Llama models (127 for 70B, 107 for 8B), while smaller models such as Qwen-7B show more uniform distributions across categories. This divergence suggests that cooperative skill abstraction is more sensitive to model capacity and architectural choices than individual skill extraction.

\subsubsection{Skill Reusability}
\label{subsec:skill_reusability}

We measure skill reusability by computing the average number of instantiations per skill type across episodes, which indicates the generalizability of learned abstractions. Figure~\ref{fig:skill_reusability} presents this analysis for both individual and cooperative skills.

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figs_append/fig3_avg_instances_per_skill.pdf}
    \caption{Average instances per skill type for individual skills (left) and cooperative skills (right). Individual skills exhibit significantly higher reuse rates (20--45$\times$), while cooperative skills span a full range from 1.0 to 24.5 instances per skill, with most skills clustered between 3 and 10 on R and H\_R tasks.}
    \label{fig:skill_reusability}
\end{figure}

Individual skills demonstrate high reusability (up to 44.8 instances per skill for Qwen-7B on Rearrange-only), with smaller models tending to extract fewer but more abstract primitives that transfer broadly. In contrast, cooperative skills exhibit substantially lower reuse rates. The full range spans from 1.0 (for HT and HRST, where data scarcity limits each skill to a single instance) to 24.5 (Qwen-7B on Rearrange-only), with most skills clustered between 3 and 10 instances on R and H\_R tasks. This reflects the inherently context-specific nature of multi-agent coordination that must adapt to varying agent configurations and spatial arrangements. The reusability gap validates our hierarchical memory design: individual skills serve as composable building blocks with broad transferability, while cooperative skills capture emergent coordination patterns requiring task-specific adaptation.

\subsubsection{Skill Quality Metrics}
\label{subsec:skill_quality}

Finally, we assess the semantic richness of extracted skills through two quality metrics: description length and precondition complexity. Figure~\ref{fig:skill_quality} presents these metrics across models and task types.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figs_append/fig8_quality_metrics.pdf}
    \caption{Skill quality metrics: description length (top row) and precondition count (bottom row) for individual and cooperative skills. Cooperative skills consistently exhibit longer descriptions reflecting coordination complexity, while precondition density varies by model architecture.}
    \label{fig:skill_quality}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Description Length.}
Cooperative skills consistently produce longer natural language descriptions (60--120 characters) compared to individual skills (30--50 characters). This difference reflects the inherent complexity of multi-agent coordination, which requires richer specification of agent roles, synchronization requirements, and interaction patterns. Llama-70B generates the most detailed cooperative descriptions (119.3 characters for HR tasks), suggesting that larger model capacity enables more comprehensive skill documentation.

\paragraph{Precondition Complexity.}
The number of preconditions per skill varies systematically across model families. Qwen models tend to generate more explicit preconditions (Qwen-72B: 2.13 for R individual skills), while Llama-70B produces sparser precondition sets (1.03 for R individual skills). This pattern suggests different extraction strategies. Qwen models favor explicit state requirements for applicability checking, while Llama models may encode more context implicitly within skill descriptions.

These quality metrics reveal a trade-off in skill representation. Richer descriptions provide better semantic grounding for retrieval-based skill selection, while explicit preconditions enable more precise applicability verification during planning. The optimal balance likely depends on the downstream planning architecture and task requirements.

\subsubsection{Qualitative Retrieval Examples}
\label{subsec:retrieval_examples}

To complement the aggregate statistics, we extract concrete retrieval cases from evaluation logs that illustrate how the hierarchical retrieval mechanism operates in practice. \textbf{(1) Task-aware boosting.} For the instruction ``move kettle from living room to kitchen, fill and turn on,'' the retrieval system returns \texttt{kettle\_operation} with combined score 1.117 (cosine similarity 0.967 plus object-type boost 0.150 from matching the ``kettle'' token). Lexical and contextual signals supplement raw embedding distance, providing discriminative power when multiple skills have similar descriptions but apply to different object classes. \textbf{(2) Decomposition into complementary skills.} For multi-object instructions such as ``move jug, kettle, and teapot to kitchen,'' top-$k$ retrieval ($k{=}5$) surfaces three complementary individual skills covering each object class plus cooperation skills capturing the handover patterns required for joint transport, rather than returning redundant variants of a single skill. \textbf{(3) Effect-based cooperation skill activation.} When the observation difference $\Delta o_t^j$ matches a stored \texttt{partner\_cond} pattern (e.g., ``object no longer on table, partner approaching kitchen''), the retrieval mechanism elevates handover-related cooperation skills above individual skills with comparable embedding similarity. Cooperation skill retrieval is thus gated by partner-induced environmental changes rather than instruction-level lexical similarity alone, realizing the effect-based partner inference mechanism described in Section~\ref{subsec:effect_inference}.

\subsection{Per-Backbone Behavior Analysis}
\label{sec:per_backbone}

The aggregate results in Section~\ref{subsec:main_results} hide a structural pattern in how memory augmentation interacts with backbone reasoning capacity. We discuss two regimes here.

\paragraph{Weak-reasoner regime: large gains, low ceiling.} On Qwen-7B, our method achieves $57.4\%$ success versus $42.1\%$ for MEMENTO ($+36\%$ relative improvement), the largest margin in our backbone comparison. Qwen-7B's baseline zero-shot success on these tasks is markedly lower than other models, indicating that intrinsic reasoning capacity is the bottleneck. In this regime, retrieved skill demonstrations supply both explicit step decomposition and partner-conditioned action templates that the backbone cannot reliably generate from instruction alone, and the hierarchical structure prevents irrelevant retrievals from overwhelming the limited in-context budget. The improvement on Qwen-7B suggests that hierarchical skill memory may be especially valuable for resource-constrained deployments where running a large model is impractical.

\paragraph{Strong-reasoner regime: success-completion divergence on GPT-4o.} GPT-4o presents the most interesting backbone behavior in our study. Our method achieves the highest \emph{completion} rate ($86.7\%$, vs.\ $84.4\%$ for ToM and $79.6\%$ for zero-shot), yet underperforms zero-shot on \emph{success rate} ($70.2\%$ vs.\ $78.7\%$). This divergence is a known PartNR pattern: completion measures the fraction of subtasks completed, while success requires \emph{all} subtasks to be completed without violating any task constraints. A method can therefore complete more subtasks while failing more often on the strict success criterion if it occasionally violates constraints (e.g., places an object on a wrong but plausible-looking surface).

We interpret the GPT-4o divergence as follows. GPT-4o has the strongest inherent zero-shot reasoning of any backbone tested ($78.7\%$ success, the highest zero-shot number), and its native ToM-prompting baseline already achieves the top success rate at $81.6\%$ (Table~\ref{tab:main_results_task}). Retrieved skill demonstrations bias the model toward the actions seen in memory, expanding subtask coverage (hence the higher completion percentage) but occasionally over-constraining the model toward an action that was successful in a similar but not identical context, breaking strict task-constraint satisfaction. This phenomenon---in-context demonstrations dominating prior reasoning when the prior is already strong---is consistent with prior observations on RAG-style augmentation of large models and suggests that our framework should be deployed selectively on backbones whose intrinsic reasoning is the limiting factor. We discuss this trade-off as a limitation in Section~\ref{sec:conclusion}.


